#{[17592186308027 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186305894] [17592186308064 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186307063] [17592186308069 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186306026] [17592186308115 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186307003] [17592186308169 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186306523] [17592186308213 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186307270] [17592186308278 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186306031] [17592186308309 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186307215] [17592186308498 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) &=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t) &=25x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 5\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1,2}=[\\pm \\text{i},5]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}(5t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}(5t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}(5t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}(5t)\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={{field-1}} \\\\ y &={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\"> <tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{\\pi }{20}$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{\\pi }{10}$</td> </tr> <tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.71$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td> </tr> <tr> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.54$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td> </tr> </tbody></table></div>\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/94/9446137440c19f9aca73c9c666089ecff5a7712ee3f389ab891a150c13ffa780.png)\n\nThe curve is \n{{field-5}}.\nSo, we have\n{{field-6}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-7}}.\n\nThe {{field-8}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/06/0678458809cf39967d7dda0d1ffbe4d43541ec309266bd08da6245ec23f6439d.png)"] [17592186308498 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}(5t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}(5t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}(5t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}(5t)\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ y &=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{20}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-0.71$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $3.54$ | $0$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/94/9446137440c19f9aca73c9c666089ecff5a7712ee3f389ab891a150c13ffa780.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)|\\le 1$ and $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)|\\le 1.$ Thus, the obtained curve is $\\text{bounded}.$ So, we have $\\text{an ellipse}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/06/0678458809cf39967d7dda0d1ffbe4d43541ec309266bd08da6245ec23f6439d.png)"] [17592186308501 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) &=-2x(t)-5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t) &=5x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-2\\pm 5\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1,2}=[\\pm \\text{i},1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}(5t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}(5t)\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={{field-1}} \\\\ y &={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\"> <tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{\\pi }{20}$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{\\pi }{10}$</td> </tr> <tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.52$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td> </tr> <tr> <td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.52$</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td> </tr> </tbody></table></div>\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/18/18f4793f4f1edc907e28b0e148510dd3c8131a544fb703d91d0a1feb0cd8a4d1.png)\n\nAs $t\\to \\mathrm{∞},$ the curve\n{{field-5}}.\nSo, we have\n{{field-6}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-7}}.\n\nThe spiral is traversed toward \n{{field-8}}\nas $t\\to \\mathrm{∞}.$\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c0/c001f8a99c09e3d1da8c6107a298989c03e64ec58c23319ce0837a757c5ddafd.png)"] [17592186308501 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}(5t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}(5t)\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ y &={e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{20}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.52$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.52$ | $0.53$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/2b/2b273edc4f977f2bee4d9a8c41128d4f2071ec39b0840610cbb7ee44492aae3e.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)|\\le 1,$ $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)|\\le 1,$ and ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, the curve $\\text{converges}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we have $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nAgain, since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c0/c001f8a99c09e3d1da8c6107a298989c03e64ec58c23319ce0837a757c5ddafd.png)"] [17592186308544 :question/problem "Given the set of functions $\\{\\hspace{0.167em}7\\operatorname{sin}x,\\hspace{0.167em}5\\operatorname{cos}x,\\hspace{0.167em}-5\\operatorname{cos}x\\}$ defined on $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.For all $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$  we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,+\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}."] [17592186308544 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the set of functions $\\{{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)\\},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have that \n\n$$\n0\\cdot 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+\\,4\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,+\\,4\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\text{not all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{dependent}.$"] [17592186308559 :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}+1,\\hspace{2em}{y}_{2}(x)=2+2{x}^{2},\\hspace{2em}x\\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  which is \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [17592186308559 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{2}+1 & 2+2{x}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)^{′} & (2+2{x}^{2})^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{2}+1 & 2+2{x}^{2} \\\\ 2x & 4x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)(4x)-(2+2{x}^{2})(2x) \\\\  & =4x({x}^{2}+1)-4x({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [17592186308561 :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=x+1,\\hspace{2em}{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2},\\hspace{2em}x\\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [17592186308561 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}x+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{′} & ({x}^{2})^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}x+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(x+1)\\cdot (2x)-{x}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2x(x+1)-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2x-{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x \\\\  & =x(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [17592186308562 :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=x,\\hspace{2em}{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}+1,\\hspace{2em}x\\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [17592186308562 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}x & {x}^{3}+1 \\\\ (x)^{′} & ({x}^{3}+1)^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}x & {x}^{3}+1 \\\\ 1 & 3{x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x\\cdot (3{x}^{2})-({x}^{3}+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}-{x}^{3}-1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [17592186308563 :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\hspace{2em}{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x},\\hspace{2em}x\\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [17592186308563 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ ({e}^{x})^{′} & ({e}^{2x})^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ {e}^{x} & 2{e}^{2x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [17592186308575 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation” is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x}-3(3{e}^{3x})+2({e}^{3x}) & =0 \\\\ 8 & =0{\\color{red}\\hspace{1em}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x}-3(2{e}^{2x})+2({e}^{2x}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation” only."] [17592186308586 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation” is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}x+4\\operatorname{cos}x)-2{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x)+5{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}x & =0 \\\\ 2(2\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x){e}^{2x} & =0{\\color{red}\\hspace{1em}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}(2x))-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}(2x))+5{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” only."] [17592186308587 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x}-6\\cdot 3{e}^{3x}+9{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x-1}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x-1} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x-1}-6\\cdot 3{e}^{3x-1}+9{e}^{3x-1} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{3x} & {e}^{3x-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{3x} & 3{e}^{3x-1}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\cdot 3{e}^{3x-1}-3{e}^{3x}\\cdot {e}^{3x-1} \\\\  & =3{e}^{6x-1}-3{e}^{6x-1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x-1}$ is a solution to the equation” only."] [17592186308589 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 2-3x\\cdot 2x+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}(x)+3)-3x(2x\\operatorname{ln}(x)+x)+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(x)+3{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(x)-3{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$” is true. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{2} & {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ 2x & 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+{x}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$”; “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$”."] [17592186308590 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{cos}(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\operatorname{cos}(2x)+16\\operatorname{cos}(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” only."] [17592186308591 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q}\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=\\mathbf{v}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{\\text{x}}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is $\\text{unbounded},$ representing $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$ Thus, we must have $a\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{the origin}.$\n\nHence, to insure that ${e}^{at}\\to 0,$ we require $a<0.$"] [17592186308592 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q}\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=\\mathbf{v}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{\\text{x}}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is $\\text{unbounded},$ representing $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$ Thus, we must have $a\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{the origin}.$\n\nHence, to insure that ${e}^{at}\\to 0,$ we require $a<0.$"] [17592186308593 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) &=4x(t)-7y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t) &=7x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =4\\pm 7\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\hspace{2em}$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}."]}
